toolUseResult.result:
Review of PR #1573 (`feat/pin-in-sync-sensor` → `refactor/slot-coordinator-owns-sync-managers`), medium effort, 8 finder angles, one verifier per candidate. Line numbers are at the PR head.

Verification tally: 13 candidates after dedup → 6 CONFIRMED, 1 PLAUSIBLE, 1 REFUTED (the "sits beside the aggregate" ordering comment is correct in production; `compareAndSortEntities` has no production caller), plus 5 minor cleanups cut by the cap. The orphaned-registry-row concern I traced myself and cleared: lock drops go through `_async_purge_dropped_locks` (includes disabled rows) and slot removal deletes the device, which HA follows with `include_disabled_entities=True`.

```json
[
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/websocket.py",
    "line": 447,
    "summary": "The new `disabled_by is None` filter on CONF_ENTITIES also drops rows the user disabled, and the legacy entities-card strategy dereferences two of those rows unconditionally, so the whole slot section throws instead of rendering.",
    "failure_scenario": "A strategy with `use_slot_cards: false` (documented option; ts/slot-section-strategy.ts:75-97) on an install where the user disabled a slot's `active` binary sensor or `credential_used` event entity in the registry. Before the PR the row arrived and rendered; after it, `getSlotMapping`'s `find` returns undefined for `pinActiveEntity`/`codeEventEntity` (typed non-optional in ts/types.ts:69,74) and `generateSlotCard` throws `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'entity_id')` at ts/generate-view.ts:258. The filter cannot distinguish 'optional, off by default' from 'required, user turned off'; either keep the payload complete and let the frontend skip rows with no `hass.states` entry, or scope the filter to `disabled_by == INTEGRATION`."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/diagnostics.py",
    "line": 39,
    "summary": "`_SENSITIVE_UNIQUE_ID_MARKERS = (\"|pin\", \"|code\")` substring-matches the new unique_id `{entry}|{slot}|pin_in_sync|{lock}`, so an enabled `pin_in_sync` sensor is redacted in diagnostics as though it held a PIN.",
    "failure_scenario": "User enables `pin_in_sync` (the only reason it exists) and downloads diagnostics to report a sync problem. `_is_sensitive` returns True (`\"|pin\" in \"e|1|pin_in_sync|lock.x\"`), so `_entity_states_for_device` (line 73) emits `state: **REDACTED**, attributes: {}` — hiding exactly the on/off and `sync_status` the download was taken to show — while the aggregate `in_sync` beside it is emitted in full. Match on the key segment (`split('|')[2] in {'pin', 'code'}`) rather than a prefix substring."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/binary_sensor.py",
    "line": 86,
    "summary": "`update_before_add=True` makes every disabled-by-default per-credential entity call `coordinator.async_request_refresh()` on the lock before HA discards it as disabled.",
    "failure_scenario": "HA's `EntityPlatform._async_add_entity` runs `entity.async_device_update()` (entity_platform.py:873-875) before the `registry_entry.disabled` abort (line 1096); `CoordinatorEntity.async_update` guards on `self.enabled`, which is True because `registry_entry` is still None. So at every entry load each `pin_in_sync` entity requests a lock usercode read and is then thrown away. The default 10 s debouncer coalesces it to at most one extra immediate/trailing refresh per lock, but it is a real Z-Wave/Matter round trip from an entity that never exists. `async_added_to_hass` already calls `_recompute()`, so drop `update_before_add` for this batch."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/const.py",
    "line": 54,
    "summary": "The four-line comment explaining `PER_LOCK_ENTITY_SUFFIX` was left at its old position when the dict moved to line 179, so it now sits above `ENTITY_IDS_RENAMED_ISSUE` and describes the wrong constant.",
    "failure_scenario": "A reader at lines 54-59 sees 'What a per-lock entity is called after the lock's name. Mirrors the entity names in strings.json... test_frontend_contract holds the two together' attached to `ENTITY_IDS_RENAMED_ISSUE = \"entity_ids_renamed\"`, which has nothing to do with strings.json; the dict's new home at 178 got a one-liner that drops the migration rationale and the name of the test that pins it. Move the original comment verbatim above the dict and delete the stranded copy."
  },
  {
    "file": "custom_components/lock_code_manager/const.py",
    "line": 179,
    "summary": "`PER_LOCK_ENTITY_SUFFIX` is a closed dict keyed by a now-generated key (`credential_in_sync_key`), and the rename migration indexes it with `[]` (__init__.py:1598), so the next managed credential type turns into a KeyError mid-migration; no test ties the two together.",
    "failure_scenario": "Latent today (`MANAGED_CREDENTIAL_TYPES == {PIN}`). Add a second type: `add_code_slot_entities` registers `<type>_in_sync` rows (disabled but in the registry, and `async_entries_for_config_entry` includes disabled rows); the next version-bump migration hits `PER_LOCK_ENTITY_SUFFIX[entity.unique_id.split('|')[2]]` and raises, aborting the rename for every remaining entity of the entry. `test_frontend_contract` iterates the dict against strings.json, not the managed types against the dict. Either build the per-type rows by comprehension over `MANAGED_CREDENTIAL_TYPES` or add a contract assertion that every `credential_in_sync_key(t)` has a suffix, a strings.json name, and an icon."
  },
  {
    "file": "ts/generate-view.ts",
    "line": 196,
    "summary": "Card naming special-cases `PIN_IN_SYNC_KEY` with a hardcoded `${lockName} PIN`, and `IN_SYNC_KEYS` in ts/const.ts is a closed hand-list, while the backend generates the key family open-endedly and the payload already carries what the frontend needs.",
    "failure_scenario": "A second credential type yields `<type>_in_sync` with no frontend change: it is not in `IN_SYNC_KEYS`, lands in `mainEntities` instead of the 'Locks in sync' fold, and `KEY_ORDER` has no slot for it. `as_partial_dict` already ships `original_name` (set at registration even for never-instantiated disabled entities, e.g. 'Front Door PIN in sync') and `entity.key` is parsed from the unique_id, so classify by `key.endsWith('_in_sync')` (mirroring `credential_in_sync_key`) and drop the per-type constant from the naming and grouping paths."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_binary_sensor.py",
    "line": 2507,
    "summary": "`test_pin_in_sync_mirrors_the_manager_once_enabled` hand-rolls `manager._state = OUT_OF_SYNC; await manager._async_tick(); await hass.async_block_till_done()`, which is exactly `async_trigger_sync_tick_for_manager(hass, manager)` in tests/conftest.py:349 (used 8 times in this file).",
    "failure_scenario": "One test drifts from the 40+ other tick-driven tests if the tick contract (e.g. the LOADING/OUT_OF_SYNC guard the helper carries) changes. Replace the three lines with the helper call inside the `patch.object(manager, \"_perform_sync\", ...)` block. Note: the preceding `codes[1] = \"0000\"` + `async_refresh()` lines are load-bearing (without them `_async_tick_impl` short-circuits to IN_SYNC before reaching `_perform_sync`), so keep those."
  },
  {
    "file": "tests/test_binary_sensor.py",
    "line": 2519,
    "summary": "`test_pin_in_sync_added_without_a_slot_coordinator_has_nothing_to_mirror` is a line-for-line copy of `test_in_sync_added_without_a_slot_coordinator_reads_unknown` (line 2441), differing only in the key, a pre-registration step, and one extra assertion.",
    "failure_scenario": "Two ~25-line tests exercise the same path (`invoke_lock_slot_adders` on slot 99, `is_on is None`, `extra_state_attributes == {}`) and have already diverged on day one: the aggregate variant lacks the `STATE_UNAVAILABLE` assertion the new one adds, though it holds for both (`has_credential` is False for slot 99 in either case). Pre-registering the aggregate enabled is a no-op (its default is enabled). Fold into one test parametrized over `[ATTR_IN_SYNC, ATTR_PIN_IN_SYNC]` with all three assertions; the next credential type then costs one list entry."
  }
]
```

Cut by the cap (all minor, unverified beyond the finder): tests/common.py:235 uses the literal `"binary_sensor"` where its sibling uses `BINARY_SENSOR_DOMAIN`; tests/test_websocket.py:161 filters on the raw substring `"pin_in_sync"` instead of `ATTR_PIN_IN_SYNC` (a key rename makes the `assert not [...]` pass vacuously); binary_sensor.py:60-84 builds the two constructions by hand with eight positional args each (a `(key, addresses, enabled)` spec tuple plus one comprehension would do); `enabled_default` could be derived from `key == ATTR_IN_SYNC` instead of being a free flag; the `IN_SYNC_KEYS` edit to `compareAndSortEntities` is test-only dead code since that comparator has no production caller.

